11.0.0 — Cycle Tones sits on the bar with Planets, Molecules and Body, and shows each calculation; this build asks one thing: make sixteen bars

New since 10079, built by the Echoel Studio run; nothing here is device-verified yet.
- Bottom bar: "Cycle Tones" stands where "Sky" stood. Its page has three lists, Planets, Molecules
  and Body, and two looks, List and Sky; the sky with the ring is the second look.
- Molecules: lines of the hydrogen atom and vibrations of everyday molecules as tones (sections
  "Hydrogen Atom" and "Everyday Molecules"), halved by whole octaves into octave 3, by the same
  arithmetic as the cycles.
- Every row: "How is this calculated?" opens the steps from the cycle to the tone, the tempo and the
  concert pitch. The tuning fork offers Key, Tempo, Key and Tempo and, last and on its own, Concert
  Pitch.
- Body: "Pulse now" shows the pulse of the heart rate strap or the rear camera, whichever is chosen
  in the Song menu, Body Source; "Take Pulse" holds it as it is at that moment, and the row below
  turns one heartbeat into a tone, a tempo and a concert pitch. A pulse from Apple Health is not
  taken. While a MIDI clock, a jam you host, OSC or the lights (Art-Net, sACN) carry the tempo off
  the device, a pulse is taken only with Share Body Data on.
- Flow: while the lights (Art-Net, sACN) are on, or a jam is hosted in any window of the app, Flow
  follows your pulse only with Share Body Data on, as it already did for a MIDI clock, OSC and a
  jam hosted in its own window.
- Not visible yet, no screen shows them: AmbiX encoding in the DSP package and a model of a
  catalogue of instrument packs in the core package.
Limits, new: a taken pulse lives only as long as the Cycle Tones page; the song keeps what the
tuning fork set. Older limits as in 10079.

Previous: 10079. A green commit that changes this file uploads build 10000 + run number. A test
list, not a source for claims (ContentPipeline/CLAIMS.md).

One ask, before any check. No build of this app has yet been used the way a musician would use
it, so this one asks for that and nothing else: start a screen recording, open the app, and try
to make sixteen bars in two to five minutes: a beat, a bass line or a chord, then Play. No list
and no protocol; stop wherever it stops being possible or pleasant. The recording is the answer.
What gets in the way in it is the next work, in the order it happened.

The five checks of 10079 stay open and unchanged. They are in this file's previous version and
rest until that recording exists.
